Major finding 2: the previous version of this log -- captured during
cycle-1 remediation -- asserted TEST-021.2 and TEST-025.2 "do not call the
real product functions at all" and recorded 17/0 (fully green, no
discrimination) for both their corresponding axes. That was accurate at the
time but went stale: a later pass (this same remediation's Major finding 1)
rewrote both assertions to dot-source resolve-component-paths.ps1 and call
its real functions directly (ConvertTo-PathStrict, Get-ChangedPaths), and
rewrote TEST-023.2 the same way (previously a source-text regex over the
script, now a direct call to the real Get-TrackedDiff with Invoke-GitRaw
monkeypatched). This file is regenerated from genuine runs against the
CURRENT suite and CURRENT product code, not hand-edited to say something
different -- every transcript below is a real, reproduced test run.

Methodology (unchanged from the prior capture): each of the 5 named
fail-closed axes (unresolvable rev, invalid UTF-8, rename-limit-exceeded,
single-writer/TOCTOU, submodule/symlink reference-only) is isolated in its
own mutate-run-revert cycle against resolve-component-paths.ps1, one axis at
a time, so each run's failure is attributable to exactly one removed check
and not to interaction between multiple simultaneous defects. `git diff
--stat` on resolve-component-paths.ps1 was confirmed empty both before this
sequence started and after every individual revert -- the shipping
implementation was never left mutated.

resolve-component-paths.ps1 is a full, independent parallel implementation
of resolve-component-paths.py (not a wrapper that shells out to Python,
INV-008 convention) -- these are genuine runs of the real PowerShell CLI
subprocess (pwsh -NoProfile -File tests/component-path-diff-basis.tests.ps1),
not a hand-edited transcript or a re-run of the bash-side log under a
different label.

Unlike the prior capture, all 5 axes below discriminate via the registered
suite -- there is no longer a registered-suite blind spot for any axis. The
suite's own baseline is now 18 assertions (DEFECT-002.1, a REQ-001
tab-indentation regression check unrelated to this task's 5 fail-closed
axes, was added in a separate prior pass and is unaffected by any mutation
below, so it stays "ok" throughout).

